# Skill: Import Module from ZIP

---

## About this skill

> **What is this skill and why does it exist?**
>
> Imagine someone hands you a ZIP file from another project — it has a full module with its own pages,
> components, tokens, and styles — and says: "We want this feature in our app." The instinct is to just
> copy the files over and tweak things until they work. That's exactly where things go wrong.
>
> You end up with duplicate Button components, one-off color variables that don't match your design system,
> or worse — you accidentally break a sidebar nav that has nothing to do with the new feature.
>
> **This skill exists to prevent that.** It gives the AI agent a strict, step-by-step process for safely
> transplanting a module from a ZIP into an existing repository. The idea is simple:
> - Take the **flow, pages, layout, and UX** from the ZIP — that stays intact.
> - Take the **tokens and components** from the *target repo* — nothing gets invented or copied from the ZIP.
>
> **When should you use this skill?**
> - You have a ZIP file (or unzipped folder) with a module/feature you want to add to a repo.
> - You're asked to "import", "port", or "transplant" a set of screens into an existing project.
> - The ZIP comes from a different codebase or design system that doesn't match the target repo 1:1.
>
> **What the agent WILL do:**
> - Add the new module's pages, routes, and flow to the target repo.
> - Preserve the UX/UI layout and flow of the module exactly as it appeared in the ZIP.
> - Replace every ZIP token and component with the closest equivalent already in the target repo.
> - Ask you before making any mapping it's not 100% sure about.
>
> **What the agent will NOT do:**
> - Touch any existing module, page, route, or flow.
> - Touch the sidebar, topbar, or any global layout element.
> - Invent new tokens or create new components.
> - Copy token or component files from the ZIP into the target repo.
> - Make judgment calls on ambiguous mappings — it will always ask first.
> - Touch any backend code, API layer, server files, or data-fetching infrastructure.
>   Data in tables and lists is always supplied as **inline dummy data** scoped to the module's
>   `data/` folder — derived from the table column structure and the module's flow, not from real APIs.

---

## Trigger

Activate this skill when the user provides a ZIP file (or unzipped folder path) containing a module,
set of pages, or feature screens and asks to integrate it into an existing repository.

---

## Golden Rules

These rules are non-negotiable and apply at every phase:

1. **Never modify existing modules, pages, routes, or flows** in the target repo.
2. **Never touch the sidebar, topbar, or any global layout/navigation** component.
3. **Never copy token definitions from the ZIP** — always find and use an existing equivalent in the target repo.
4. **Never copy component implementations from the ZIP** — always map to an existing component in the target repo.
5. **Never invent new tokens or create new components.** Reuse only what already exists.
6. **Preserve the module's UX/UI layout and flow exactly** — structure, page order, and user journey must remain identical.
7. **When in doubt, stop and ask the user.** Never make a judgment call on a mapping you are not confident about.
8. **Never use raw hex/rgba/px values** for design intent — every visual value must trace to a token from the target repo's design system.
9. **Never touch the backend.** Do not create, edit, or delete any server-side files, API routes, database schemas, or data-fetching infrastructure. The module is a frontend prototype only.
10. **All data in tables and lists is dummy data.** Every table column and list item must be populated with realistic-looking static data placed in `src/<module>/data/`. Derive field names and value shapes from the table column definitions and the module's user flow — not from a real API contract.
11. **Hydra prototype: follow the BOM project model.** Construction project selection is never inside an imported module. Use `useActiveProject()` (`src/context/ActiveProjectContext.tsx`) as the single source of truth — same as `BomLandingPage`. Skip ZIP project-list / project-create entry screens. Project switching stays in the top bar (`AppTopBar`) — do not modify it or duplicate project selection in the module.
12. **Visual polish is part of done.** Before closing, run Phase **5h** and the Phase 6 **Visual polish** checklist in the browser. Antd defaults often cause overlap, duplicate chrome, and wrong typography — reconcile with ZIP layout *behaviour* using repo tokens and typography roles.
13. **No duplicate UI on screen.** The ZIP standalone app repeats chrome (project name, nav, lists) that the Hydra shell already provides. When porting, **drop or merge** — never render the same label, CTA, picker, or status block twice on one viewport. One owner per piece of chrome (see Phase **2f** and **5h-B**).

---

## Phase 0 — Repo Pre-flight (Hydra-specific)

> This phase applies specifically to the **Hydra on-hydra-app-prototype** repository.
> If the target repo is different, adapt accordingly using Phase 1.

Before touching the ZIP, orient yourself to the Hydra system:

### Token system

All design tokens ultimately trace to these files under `src/theme/`:

| File | What it owns |
|------|-------------|
| `antdTheme.ts` | Master Ant Design `ThemeConfig` — `hydraAntdTheme` (light) and `hydraAntdThemeDark` |
| `hydraAlias.ts` | `hydraLightAlias`, `hydraDarkAlias`, **`shellLayout`** (header height, sider width, etc.) |
| `hydraSemanticColors.ts` | Neutral / dark-menu chrome literals |
| `primaryPrimitiveBlue.ts` | `Primary/primary-*` ramps (blue) |
| `primaryPrimitiveOrange.ts` | `Volcano/volcano-*` ramps (orange) |
| `buttonPrimaryPalette.ts` | Orange solid Button palette |
| `sidebarSemantics.ts` | Sidebar-specific color semantics |
| `hydraTypography.ts` | Named text-style helpers (e.g. `hydraSmallStrong`) |
| `hydraAntdTypography.ts` | antd typography token (font family, sizes) |

**In components:** always obtain tokens via `theme.useToken()` from antd, `shellLayout`, or imports from the
files above — never paste raw values.

### Component barrel

All antd components and Hydra-specific UI components are re-exported from **`src/ui/antd.ts`**.
When porting pages, import components from `@/ui/antd` (or the relative path to `src/ui/antd.ts`),
never directly from `antd`. Check `src/ui/antd.ts` to confirm a component is exported before using it.
If it is not yet in the barrel, **stop and ask the user** whether to add it.

### Module folder convention

Follow the `src/bom/` module as the canonical example:

```
src/<module>/
  <Module>Routes.tsx       ← module-level nested Routes (uses react-router-dom)
  <module>State.ts         ← global state / machine for the module (if needed)
  components/              ← module-specific presentational components
  pages/                   ← one file per route segment
  layout/                  ← module layout wrapper (e.g. BomLayout.tsx) used as Route element
  hooks/                   ← module-specific hooks
  context/                 ← module-specific React context
  constants/               ← enums, constants
  data/                    ← mock/static data, fixtures
```

### Routing convention

- `src/App.tsx` mounts top-level routes inside `<AppShell />`.
- Module routes are nested via a single `<Route path="/<module>/*" element={<ModuleRoutes />} />` entry
  in `App.tsx`.
- The module's own `<ModuleRoutes />` component uses a `<BomLayout>`-style wrapper as the outer
  `<Route element={...}>` to share layout across pages.
- **Never modify existing `<Route>` entries** in `App.tsx`. Only append the new module's entry.

### Active project context — follow `src/bom/` (canonical)

In **on-hydra-app-prototype**, every imported module scopes work to the **active construction project**
the same way **`BomLandingPage`** does. Read that file before porting any module.

**Reference implementation:** `src/bom/pages/BomLandingPage.tsx`

#### Two levels of “project” (do not conflate)

| Level | What it is | Example (BOM) | Who owns selection |
|-------|------------|-----------------|-------------------|
| **Construction project** | Top-level job site / shell project | `projectId` from `useActiveProject()` | **Top bar only** (`AppTopBar`) |
| **Module entity** | Records inside the module for that job | BOM projects (`mockBomProjects`, route `:bomProjectId`) | Module landing + deep routes |

ZIP apps often blur these (e.g. a construction `ProjectListPage` as module entry). In the prototype,
**drop construction-level list/create screens** from the ZIP. The module opens on its **own landing
view** for the already-selected construction project.

#### Required patterns (copy from BOM)

1. **Read construction project from context** — not from an in-module picker:

   ```tsx
   const { projectId, project } = useActiveProject();
   ```

2. **Filter mock data by construction `projectId`** — every fixture row carries a `projectId` field:

   ```tsx
   const scopedRows = useMemo(
     () => mockModuleEntities.filter((row) => row.projectId === projectId),
     [projectId],
   );
   ```

   See `mockBomProjects` in `src/bom/data/mockData.ts`.

3. **Reset local UI state when `projectId` changes** (selection, search, pagination):

   ```tsx
   useEffect(() => {
     setSelectedRowKeys([]);
     setSearchText('');
     setPage(1);
   }, [projectId]);
   ```

4. **No construction project selected** — show an `Alert` pointing to the top bar (do not build a
   picker). See BOM: *“Pick a project from the top bar to scope BOM runs…”*

5. **Module-specific IDs in deep routes** — construction `projectId` stays in context; route params
   name module entities (BOM: `:bomProjectId`; SeisBrace: `:seismicDesignId`, `:braceId`, etc.).

6. **Routes under `/projects/:projectId/…`** — `ProjectWorkspaceLayout` syncs URL →
   `ActiveProjectContext`; still call `useActiveProject()` in pages (do not rely on URL alone).

#### What to skip from the ZIP

- `ProjectListPage`, `CreateEditFormPage`, or any **construction** project list / create / edit as
  module entry — shell `/projects` and the top bar own that.
- In-module construction project dropdowns or switchers.

#### What the module landing may still show

The **first screen after import** is the module’s own content for the active construction project —
not a construction project picker. Examples:

- **BOM:** table of BOM projects for `projectId` (`/bom` index).
- **SeisBrace:** designs + brace table for `projectId` (ZIP `ProjectDetailPage` content).

Mock data under `src/<module>/data/` must include `projectId` on every construction-scoped row so the
top-bar switcher shows distinct fixtures per job site.

### Read-only shell elements

Mark these as **untouchable** for this task:
- `src/shell/AppShell.tsx` and everything under `src/shell/`
- `src/context/`
- `src/theme/` (read only — do not add or modify token files)
- `src/ui/antd.ts` (read only unless adding a missing antd export, which requires user approval)

